    // Stored as chunked string literals rather than one intArrayOf(...) of
    // every character's code point -- see buildKotlinSource in lawgpt.html
    // for why.
    private val textChunks: List<String> = listOf(
        "[0m[1m1. Rules[0m\n[0m[1mRule: Time-of-filing rule for diversity jurisdiction[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding; ConnectU quotes Grupo Dataflux v. Atlas\nGlobal Group, L.P., 541 U.S. 567, 570–571 (2004), which in turn\nquotes Mollan v. Torrance, 22 U.S. (9 Wheat.) 537, 539 (1824)\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 877-83\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: \"The jurisdiction of the Court depends upon the\nstate of things at the time of the action brought,\" so every\nchallenge to subject-matter jurisdiction based on diversity of\ncitizenship is measured \"against the state of facts that existed\nat the time of filing\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: At what point in time does a court assess the\nparties' citizenship when deciding whether diversity\njurisdiction exists?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. Identify the date the action was filed (here, September\n    2, 2004, the filing date of the original complaint)\n    2. Determine each relevant party's citizenship as of that\n    date, without regard to later changes\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Courts use this rule whenever a party challenges\ndiversity jurisdiction; in ConnectU, it limited the\njurisdictional discovery and the evidentiary hearing to\nZuckerberg's citizenship \"at the time that the original\ncomplaint was filed,\" so the facts that came after filing (his\nlater California leases, his 2006 California license, his lack\nof plans to return to Harvard) mattered only to the extent they\nshed light on where he was domiciled on September 2, 2004\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The reading states no exceptions; Grupo Dataflux\ndescribes the rule as applying to \"all challenges\" based on\ndiversity, \"whether the challenge be brought shortly after\nfiling, after the trial, or even for the first time on appeal,\"\nso the timing of the challenge does not change the date used for\nmeasurement\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: This rule fixes when citizenship is measured,\nwhile the domicile rules below fix how citizenship is\ndetermined; a change of domicile after filing, which is the\nquestion raised in Note 2, does not create or destroy\njurisdiction under this rule\n\n[0m[1mRule: Timing of challenges to diversity jurisdiction[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding; Grupo Dataflux v. Atlas Global Group,\nL.P., 541 U.S. 567, 570–571 (2004), as quoted in ConnectU\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 877-83\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: A challenge to subject-matter jurisdiction based on\ndiversity may be brought \"shortly after filing, after the trial,\nor even for the first time on appeal,\" and in every case it is\nmeasured against the facts at the time of filing\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: When may a party challenge the existence of\ndiversity jurisdiction?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: The challenge must go to the subject-matter\njurisdiction of the federal court, as distinct from some other\ndefense\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: A defendant can raise lack of diversity at any\nstage; here the Facebook defendants raised it in a pretrial\nmotion to dismiss, but the quoted language shows the same\nchallenge would remain open later in the case\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The reading states none\n\n[0m[1mRule: Rule 12(b)(1) motion to dismiss for lack of subject matter[0m\n[0m[1mjurisdiction[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 12(b)(1), as invoked\nin ConnectU\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 877-83\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: A defendant may move to dismiss a complaint for\nlack of subject matter jurisdiction under Rule 12(b)(1),\nincluding on the ground that the diversity of citizenship\nalleged in the complaint does not exist as a matter of fact\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: How does a defendant raise the argument that a\nfederal court lacks subject matter jurisdiction?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. A motion to dismiss asserting lack of subject matter\n    jurisdiction\n    2. A ground for the challenge, here that jurisdiction was\n    \"alleged to be premised solely upon diversity\" but \"as a\n    matter of fact, diversity did not exist\"\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: The Facebook defendants used Rule 12(b)(1) to\nattack the factual basis for jurisdiction; they did not argue\nthat the complaint's allegations were inadequate on their face\nbut that the actual citizenship facts defeated diversity, and\nthat factual dispute is what led to discovery and an evidentiary\nhearing\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The reading does not discuss any limits on this\nmotion\n\n[0m[1mRule: Fact-finding on disputed jurisdictional facts[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Inferred from the court's procedure in ConnectU\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 877-83\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: When the parties dispute the facts that\njurisdiction depends on, such as a party's domicile, the court\nmay allow limited discovery on that question, hold an\nevidentiary hearing, and make its own factual findings,\nincluding credibility findings\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: How does a federal court resolve a factual\ndispute over whether diversity of citizenship exists?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. A genuine dispute over a jurisdictional fact (here,\n    Zuckerberg's domicile)\n    2. Discovery limited to that jurisdictional question (here,\n    Zuckerberg's citizenship when the original complaint was\n    filed)\n    3. An evidentiary hearing at which the court weighs\n    testimony and documents (held June 22, 2006)\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: The magistrate judge relied on deposition and\nhearing testimony and stated that the court \"credits\"\nZuckerberg's testimony about his intent; this shows that the\ncourt itself decides jurisdictional facts and that credibility\ncan decide the outcome\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The reading states none\n\n[0m[1mRule: Definition of domicile[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding; standard quoted and applied in ConnectU\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 877-83\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: A person's domicile is \"the place where he has a\ntrue, fixed home and principal establishment, and to which,\nwhenever he is absent he has the intention of returning\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Which state is an individual a citizen of for\npurposes of diversity jurisdiction?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. A true, fixed home and principal establishment in the\n    state\n    2. An intention of returning to that place whenever absent\n    from it, which can also be described as an intent to remain\n    there\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: The court found that on September 2, 2004,\nCalifornia was not Zuckerberg's \"true, fixed home and principal\nestablishment,\" because his credited intent at that time was to\nstay in California for \"a semester or maybe two\" and then go\nback to Harvard, so his domicile stayed in New York\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: Physical residence by itself does not satisfy the\ntest (see the rule that residence alone is not domicile)\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Domicile is different from residence; a person\ncan live in one state for months, as Zuckerberg did in\nCalifornia, while remaining domiciled in another\n\n[0m[1mRule: Factors indicating intent to remain in a state[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding; factors listed by the ConnectU court\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 877-83\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Relevant factors include \"the place where civil and\npolitical rights are exercised, taxes paid, real and personal\nproperty (such as furniture and automobiles) located, driver's\nand other licenses obtained, bank accounts maintained, location\nof club and church membership and places of business or\nemployment\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: What evidence shows whether a person intends\nto remain in a state, which is the intent element of domicile?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. Place where civil and political rights are exercised,\n    such as voting\n    2. Place where taxes are paid\n    3. Location of real and personal property, such as furniture\n    and automobiles\n    4. Place where driver's and other licenses are obtained\n    5. Location of bank accounts\n    6. Location of club and church memberships\n    7. Places of business or employment\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: The court weighed each factor and did not simply\ncount them, as the following show:\n    - Voting: New York registration got \"little weight\" because\n    Zuckerberg had never voted and did not remember registering\n    - Licenses: he held a New York driver's license from age 16\n    until about two months before the 2006 hearing, which\n    pointed to New York\n    - Bank accounts: in 2004 all his personal accounts and\n    credit cards were New York accounts, and the statements went\n    to Dobbs Ferry, which pointed to New York\n    - Personal property: he brought one bag to California and\n    left the rest in storage at Harvard or at his parents'\n    house, and the Westbrook house had only \"bare essentials\"\n    for furniture, which pointed away from California\n    - Automobiles: he did not remember whether he registered the\n    SUV he bought online during the sublet, and he did not lease\n    and register an Infiniti in his own name until December\n    2004, after the filing date\n    - Taxes: his 2004 federal return had been prepared but not\n    filed, and as of the hearing no state return for 2004 had\n    been filed, so this factor gave little help\n    - Business: TheFacebook Inc. was incorporated by California\n    attorneys on July 29, 2004, and the site's servers moved to\n    California, which pointed toward California\n    - Addresses: he \"routinely\" gave Dobbs Ferry as his\n    permanent address, as on the La Jennifer Way lease and the\n    Equinex contract, and the California addresses on\n    incorporation documents got less weight because others\n    filled in those forms and he \"attributed no importance\" to\n    them\n- [0m[1mApplication (continued)[0m: Business activity in a state does not\nsettle domicile when the personal-life factors and credited\ntestimony about intent point elsewhere\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: A factor can be discounted when the evidence\nbehind it is weak, as with registration without ever voting or\naddresses entered on forms by other people\n\n[0m[1mRule: Residence alone is not domicile[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Textbook narrative; the casebook's margin summary of\nConnectU\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 877-83\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: \"Residence alone does not amount to domicile\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Does living in a state make a person domiciled\nthere?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: To become a domicile, residence must be combined\nwith the required intent to remain, meaning the place must be\nthe person's true, fixed home to which they intend to return\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Zuckerberg was living in Palo Alto, California,\non September 2, 2004, but his residence there was a summer\nsublet he shared with student friends while planning to go back\nto Harvard, so living there did not make California his domicile\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The reading states none\n\n[0m[1mRule: Persistence of domicile[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Textbook narrative; the casebook's margin summary of\nConnectU\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 877-83\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: \"A person remains domiciled in a state until he\nestablishes domicile elsewhere\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Where is a person domiciled when they have\nleft their old home but have not yet settled in a new one?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. An existing domicile, here New York, where Zuckerberg was\n    born, went to school until mid-high school, and where his\n    family home was\n    2. No new domicile yet formed, meaning residence plus intent\n    to remain has not come together in another state\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Zuckerberg had left New York for Exeter, Harvard,\nand California, but because he had not formed a California\ndomicile by September 2, 2004, the court treated him as still\ndomiciled in New York; living in New Hampshire and Massachusetts\nfor school did not displace New York either\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The reading states none\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: This rule works together with the time-of-filing\nrule; the old domicile controls at filing even if the person\nlater forms a new domicile, which is the issue raised in Note 2\n\n[0m[1mRule: Citizenship of the parties as the basis of diversity[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Inferred from ConnectU and Notes and Questions 1\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 877-83\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Diversity jurisdiction depends on the parties'\ncitizenship at the time of filing, and the defendants argued\nthat diversity \"did not exist\" where the plaintiff, ConnectU,\nand a defendant, Zuckerberg, were both citizens of New York\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Does a federal court have diversity\njurisdiction over the action?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. A determination of the plaintiff's citizenship, here\n    ConnectU as a citizen of New York on September 2, 2004\n    2. A determination of each defendant's citizenship, here\n    Zuckerberg as domiciled in New York\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: The court's findings placed a plaintiff and a\ndefendant in the same state, which is the basis of the\ndefendants' argument that diversity did not exist\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: Note 1 points out that the court made no findings\nabout TheFacebook, Inc.'s citizenship and did not decide whether\nConnectU LLC was also a citizen of some state other than New\nYork, and it says that entities such as LLCs \"can be citizens of\nmore than one state at a given time\"; the attached reading does\nnot explain the rules for the citizenship of corporations or\nLLCs, which the casebook says come \"below\"\n\n[0m[1m2. Case Narrative[0m\nConnectU LLC v. Zuckerberg is a lawsuit brought by ConnectU LLC,\na business entity, against Mark Zuckerberg, the founder of\nFacebook, and TheFacebook, Inc., the company he formed. ConnectU\nis the plaintiff, and Zuckerberg and Facebook (the \"Facebook\nDefendants\") are the defendants. The excerpt says nothing about\nwhat ConnectU does or what the dispute between ConnectU and\nFacebook was about. Note 3 says only that this case was one\n\"strand in a web of litigation\" among ConnectU, Facebook, and\ntheir founders over several years, and that Facebook separately\nsued ConnectU's founders in California.\nThe facts that matter in the excerpt are about Zuckerberg's life\nand where he lived. He was born in White Plains, New York, and\nattended elementary school and his first two years of high\nschool in Ardsley, New York. His family home is at 2 Russell\nPlace, Dobbs Ferry, New York. In 2000, for his junior year of\nhigh school, he went to Phillips Exeter in New Hampshire and\nlived on campus. He was away the summer after his senior year,\ndid not live with his parents the summer after his freshman year\nat Harvard, and spent the summer after his sophomore year in\nCalifornia. He was 22 at the time of the June 2006 hearing, so\nhe was about 20 in 2004.\nAt the end of the spring 2004 term at Harvard, Zuckerberg put\nhis belongings in storage at Kirkland House, his dorm, because\nhe planned to return in the fall. He spent two or three weeks at\nhis parents' home in Dobbs Ferry. Around mid-June 2004 he went\nto Palo Alto, California, with one bag of belongings. He moved\ninto a sublet at 819 La Jennifer Way that he shared with five\nother student friends. He went to California partly because that\nwas where start-up companies were. He wanted to \"hang out\" with\nfriends and work on expanding the Facebook to other schools. He\nthought of California as a \"cool place\" to spend the summer\nbefore going back to Harvard, and he was not thinking about\nstaying. The La Jennifer Way lease listed his Dobbs Ferry\naddress, and he gave his New York address whenever he needed a\npermanent address. Harvard granted him a leave of absence\neffective May 28, 2004, and he cancelled his fall housing,\nintending to be away for a term or two. He still wanted to\ngraduate from Harvard.\nThe business grew over the summer. The California attorneys\nZuckerberg hired incorporated TheFacebook Inc. on July 29, 2004\n(he signed the certificate on July 26). Around August 8, 2004,\nhe signed an agreement, using his New York address, with\nEquinex, a co-location facility in California. In late August or\nSeptember he moved the site's data from Savvy, a New York\nco-location facility, to Equinex, and after that the site was\nrun from California. By mid to late August he expected about\n\$550,000 in funding. That summer he spent a good part of the\nmoney his parents had saved for his college education, and he\npaid it back soon after venture capital came in in September. As\nof September 2, 2004, the date ConnectU filed its complaint,\nFacebook had not yet received venture funding. Zuckerberg's plan\non that date was to stay in California for a term or two, see\nhow Facebook did, and then return to Harvard. Facebook received\nits first venture funding sometime before September 11. At the\nend of August it was in fewer than fifty schools, about one\nhundred more were added after the move to Equinex was finished\naround the second week of September, and it had a million users\nby December 1 or 2, 2004. Zuckerberg admitted he had been wrong\nto think he could run the company and go back to school.\nWhen the La Jennifer Way lease ended around September 11 or 12,\n2004, Zuckerberg spent a few days at his family home in New\nYork, intending to return to California \"for a short period of\ntime.\" From mid-September 2004 to mid-January 2005 he lived in\nan unfurnished house at 1743 Westbrook Avenue in Los Altos. He\nsigned the lease, Facebook paid the rent, and they took a\none-year lease because nothing else was available. The\nhousemates bought minimal furniture and split the cost. He had\nno dresser and piled up his clothes. He opened gas, electric,\nand cable accounts in his own name. He moved to 1324 Sherman\nAvenue in Menlo Park from mid-January to about mid-June 2005\nbecause a roommate was having an allergic reaction to something\nin the Westbrook house. He spent summer 2005 at Vasilakos Court\nand moved into an unfurnished apartment on Ramona Street in Palo\nAlto in fall 2005. Apart from a few weeks, he lived in\nCalifornia from summer 2004 on, and by the 2006 hearing he had\nno plans to return to Harvard.\nOther facts relate to his ties to New York. In 2004 he had a\nstudent checking account at Fleet/Bank of America in New York, a\njoint savings account with his mother at Smith Barney in New\nYork, a debit card, and a joint Citibank MasterCard with his\nfather, and all the statements went to Dobbs Ferry. After he\nmoved to California, his parents paid only for his cell phone\nand his health insurance, until Facebook took over the premiums.\nHe bought an SUV online during the sublet but could not remember\nwhether it was registered or in whose name. He leased and\nregistered an Infiniti in his own name in December 2004. He held\na New York driver's license from age 16 until about two months\nbefore the 2006 hearing, when he got a California license. He\nwas registered to vote in New York but had never voted and did\nnot remember registering. His 2004 federal tax return showed\n\$19,783 in income: \$19,333 from Facebook, all earned after\nincorporation, and \$450 from Harvard. The return had been\nprepared by his father's accountant but not filed, and no state\nreturn for 2004 had been filed. The excerpt says ConnectU was a\ncitizen of New York as of September 2, 2004.\nOn September 2, 2004, ConnectU sued Zuckerberg and Facebook in\nfederal court in Massachusetts. The original complaint relied\nonly on diversity of citizenship for federal jurisdiction. The\nexcerpt does not say what claims ConnectU brought or what relief\nit wanted.\n\n+--------------+--------------+--------------+--------------+\n| [0m[1mParty[0m        | [0m[1mWhere it[0m     | [0m[1mWhere it[0m     | [0m[1mWhy[0m          |\n|              | [0m[1mwanted[0m       | [0m[1mwanted[0m       |              |\n|              | [0m[1mConnectU's[0m   | [0m[1mZuckerberg's[0m |              |\n|              | [0m[1mcitizenship[0m  | [0m[1mcitizenship[0m  |              |\n+--------------+--------------+--------------+------------",
        "--+\n| [0m[1mConnectU[0m     | In a state   | California   | ConnectU's   |\n| [0m[1m(plaintiff)[0m  | other than   |              | complaint    |\n|              | Zuckerberg's |              | relied only  |\n|              | (the court   |              | on           |\n|              | found        |              | diversity,   |\n|              | ConnectU was |              | so it needed |\n|              | a New York   |              | the two      |\n|              | citizen)     |              | parties to   |\n|              |              |              | be citizens  |\n|              |              |              | of different |\n|              |              |              | states; with |\n|              |              |              | ConnectU in  |\n|              |              |              | New York,    |\n|              |              |              | that meant   |\n|              |              |              | Zuckerberg   |\n|              |              |              | had to be a  |\n|              |              |              | California   |\n|              |              |              | domiciliary  |\n|              |              |              | on September |\n|              |              |              | 2, 2004      |\n+--------------+--------------+--------------+--------------+\n| [0m[1mZuckerberg[0m   | New York     | New York     | Their Rule   |\n| [0m[1mand Facebook[0m |              |              | 12(b)(1)     |\n| [0m[1m(defendants)[0m |              |              | motion       |\n|              |              |              | argued that  |\n|              |              |              | \"diversity   |\n|              |              |              | did not      |\n|              |              |              | exist,\" so   |\n|              |              |              | they wanted  |\n|              |              |              | ConnectU and |\n|              |              |              | Zuckerberg   |\n|              |              |              | to be        |\n|              |              |              | citizens of  |\n|              |              |              | the same     |\n|              |              |              | state, which |\n|              |              |              | would mean   |\n|              |              |              | dismissal    |\n|              |              |              | for lack of  |\n|              |              |              | subject      |\n|              |              |              | matter       |\n|              |              |              | jurisdiction |\n+--------------+--------------+--------------+--------------+\n\n[0m[1m3. Cases[0m\n[0m[1mCase Name: ConnectU LLC v. Zuckerberg[0m\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 877-83\n- [0m[1mCourse[0m: Civil Procedure (Class 24, Introduction to Subject\nMatter Jurisdiction; Diversity Jurisdiction)\n- [0m[1mFacts[0m\n    - ConnectU LLC filed its original complaint on September 2,\n    2004, and relied only on diversity for federal jurisdiction\n    - The court found that ConnectU was a citizen of New York on\n    September 2, 2004\n    - Zuckerberg was born and raised in New York, his family\n    home is in Dobbs Ferry, New York, and he attended Exeter in\n    New Hampshire and then Harvard in Massachusetts\n    - After the spring 2004 term, he stored his belongings in\n    his Harvard dorm, got a leave of absence effective May 28,\n    2004, and in mid-June 2004 moved with one bag into a shared\n    student summer sublet in Palo Alto, California\n    - On September 2, 2004, he intended to stay in California\n    \"for a semester or maybe two\" and then return to Harvard,\n    and he did not intend to drop out\n    - TheFacebook Inc. was incorporated on July 29, 2004, by\n    California attorneys, and the servers moved from New York to\n    Equinex in California in late August or September 2004\n    - Venture funding arrived after September 2 and before\n    September 11, 2004, and the site then grew \"exponentially,\"\n    reaching a million users by early December 2004\n    - Zuckerberg routinely gave Dobbs Ferry as his permanent\n    address, and in 2004 he held a New York driver's license and\n    had only New York bank accounts and credit cards\n    - He was registered to vote in New York but had never voted\n    and did not remember registering\n    - Facts after filing point toward California: a one-year\n    lease on an unfurnished Los Altos house starting\n    mid-September 2004, an Infiniti registered in his name in\n    December 2004, several later California homes, a California\n    license in 2006, and no plans to return to Harvard as of the\n    hearing\n    - The court allowed limited discovery and held an\n    evidentiary hearing on June 22, 2006, after Zuckerberg's\n    deposition on April 25, 2006\n- [0m[1mProcedural Posture[0m:\n    - U.S. District Court for the District of Massachusetts:\n    ConnectU LLC sued Mark Zuckerberg and TheFacebook, Inc., and\n    the case is before the court on the Facebook Defendants'\n    motion to dismiss under Rule 12(b)(1) for lack of subject\n    matter jurisdiction, referred to Magistrate Judge Robert B.\n    Collings under 28 U.S.C. § 636(b)(1)(B) for a Report and\n    Recommendation\n- [0m[1mIssue[0m: Whether Mark Zuckerberg was domiciled in New York or in\nCalifornia on September 2, 2004, the date the complaint was\nfiled, which decides whether diversity of citizenship existed\nbetween him and ConnectU, a New York citizen?\n- [0m[1mHolding[0m: The U.S. District Court for the District of\nMassachusetts, through Magistrate Judge Collings's Report and\nRecommendation as adopted by District Judge Woodlock, held that\non September 2, 2004, Zuckerberg was domiciled in New York and\nnot California, because California was not then his \"true, fixed\nhome and principal establishment\" to which he intended to return\n- [0m[1mRule[0m:\n    - Time-of-filing rule for diversity jurisdiction: Diversity\n    jurisdiction is measured \"against the state of facts that\n    existed at the time of filing\"\n    - Definition of domicile: Domicile is \"the place where he\n    has a true, fixed home and principal establishment, and to\n    which, whenever he is absent he has the intention of\n    returning\"\n    - Factors indicating intent to remain in a state: Intent is\n    shown by where civil and political rights are exercised,\n    taxes are paid, property is located, licenses are obtained,\n    bank accounts are kept, memberships are held, and business\n    or employment takes place\n    - Residence alone is not domicile: Physical residence in a\n    state, without the required intent, does not establish\n    domicile\n    - Persistence of domicile: A person remains domiciled in a\n    state until he establishes domicile elsewhere\n    - Fact-finding on disputed jurisdictional facts: The court\n    may allow limited discovery and hold an evidentiary hearing\n    to decide disputed jurisdictional facts, including the\n    credibility of testimony about intent\n- [0m[1mReasoning[0m: The district court reasoned in these steps:\n    - Under Grupo Dataflux, citizenship is fixed as of September\n    2, 2004, so discovery and the hearing were limited to\n    Zuckerberg's domicile on that date\n    - In 2004 Zuckerberg was a 20-year-old student who went to\n    California to spend a summer \"hanging out\" with friends in\n    \"the mecca of the start-up world,\" lived in a shared summer\n    sublet, and brought only one bag, while the rest of his\n    belongings stayed at Harvard or in New York\n    - Even after he moved into the unfurnished Westbrook house\n    in mid-September, the housemates bought only \"bare\n    essentials,\" which shows how temporary the arrangement was\n    - He \"routinely\" gave Dobbs Ferry as his permanent address,\n    as on the La Jennifer Way lease and the Equinex contract,\n    and the California addresses on incorporation documents\n    carried less weight because others completed those forms and\n    he attributed no importance to them\n    - His New York driver's license, held until 2006, and his\n    New York bank accounts and credit cards in 2004 pointed to\n    New York\n    - His New York voter registration got \"little weight\"\n    because he never voted and did not remember registering\n    - The Infiniti was not leased and registered in his name\n    until December 2004, after the filing date, and he could not\n    remember how the earlier SUV was registered\n    - The court credited his testimony that in September 2004 he\n    intended to stay in California for a semester or two and\n    then return to Harvard\n    - Facebook only \"began to take off exponentially\" after the\n    capital infusion and the move to Equinex in mid to late\n    September 2004, so \"the landscape changed\" after the filing\n    date, and later facts could not move his domicile backward\n    to September 2\n    - California therefore was not his true, fixed home on\n    September 2, 2004, and his New York domicile continued\n- [0m[1mPlaintiff's/Appellee's Arguments[0m: The excerpt says the parties\ndisputed Zuckerberg's domicile but does not set out ConnectU's\narguments; to support jurisdiction, ConnectU would have needed\nZuckerberg to be domiciled outside New York, and the facts in\nthe record that support California as his domicile are:\n    - He had lived in California since mid-June 2004 and ran\n    Facebook from there\n    - TheFacebook Inc. was incorporated by California attorneys\n    in July 2004, and the servers moved to a California facility\n    - California addresses appeared on incorporation documents\n    - He bought an SUV while in California\n    - He signed a one-year lease on the Westbrook house in\n    mid-September 2004 and opened utility accounts in his own\n    name\n- [0m[1mDefendant/Appellant's Arguments[0m: The Facebook Defendants\nargued for dismissal under Rule 12(b)(1):\n    - The original complaint relied only on diversity, but \"as a\n    matter of fact, diversity did not exist\"\n    - Zuckerberg was domiciled in New York when the complaint\n    was filed, the same state as ConnectU, as shown by his\n    intent to return to Harvard, his permanent New York address,\n    his New York license, his New York bank accounts and credit\n    cards, and the temporary nature of his California living\n    arrangements\n- [0m[1mDisposition[0m: The U.S. District Court for the District of\nMassachusetts, through Judge Woodlock's order adopting\nMagistrate Judge Collings's Report and Recommendation, found\nthat Zuckerberg was domiciled in New York on September 2, 2004;\nthe excerpt ends with that finding and does not reproduce the\nfinal recommendation on the motion; the case caption notes that\nthe First Circuit later reversed \"on other grounds,\" 522 F.3d 82\n(1st Cir. 2008), and the reading does not explain those grounds\n- [0m[1mImportance[0m: The case shows how the domicile test applies to\nsomeone in transition, and these are the main lessons:\n    - Domicile depends on intent, and credible testimony about\n    intent can outweigh months of physical presence and\n    significant business activity in another state\n    - The time-of-filing rule decides the case: Zuckerberg's\n    life moved to California within weeks of the filing date,\n    but what counts is his intent on September 2, 2004\n    - Courts weigh the factors qualitatively; voting\n    registration without voting, and addresses filled in by\n    other people, get little weight\n    - The textbook's two takeaways are that \"residence alone\n    does not amount to domicile\" and that \"a person remains\n    domiciled in a state until he establishes domicile\n    elsewhere\"\n    - Note 1 for class: the findings may not fully decide\n    diversity because they do not address TheFacebook, Inc.'s\n    citizenship or whether ConnectU LLC was also a citizen of\n    another state; entity citizenship is covered later in the\n    casebook and not in this reading\n    - Note 2 for class: the facts after filing (mid-September\n    capital infusion, the one-year Westbrook lease, utilities in\n    his name, the December 2004 Infiniti, later California\n    homes, the 2006 California license, and no plans to return\n    to Harvard) are the evidence for arguing about when his\n    domicile moved to California; the court did not decide that\n    question\n\n[0m[1m4. Definitions[0m\n[0m[1mSubject matter jurisdiction[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A court's power to hear a particular kind of case;\ndiversity of citizenship is one basis for federal subject matter\njurisdiction\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: The Facebook Defendants' motion challenged the\ncourt's subject matter jurisdiction because the only basis\nalleged was diversity\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: This differs from a challenge to the merits of\nthe claims, since it asks whether the court can hear the case at\nall\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: ConnectU LLC v. Zuckerberg\n\n[0m[1mDiversity jurisdiction[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Federal jurisdiction based on the parties being\ncitizens of different states, measured as of the time the action\nis filed\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: ConnectU's original complaint relied \"solely\nupon diversity,\" so the case depended on whether Zuckerberg and\nConnectU were citizens of different states on September 2, 2004\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: The attached reading does not cover other bases\nof federal jurisdiction or the detailed requirements of\ndiversity beyond citizenship\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: ConnectU LLC v. Zuckerberg\n\n[0m[1mDomicile[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: \"The place where he has a true, fixed home and\nprincipal establishment, and to which, whenever he is absent he\nhas the intention of returning\"\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: An individual's domicile determines that\nperson's state citizenship for diversity purposes, and the case\nturned on whether Zuckerberg's domicile was New York or\nCalifornia\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Domicile requires intent in addition to presence,\nand it continues until a new one is established, while residence\nis only where a person currently lives\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: ConnectU LLC v. Zuckerberg\n\n[0m[1mResidence[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The place where a person is actually living at a\ngiven time\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Zuckerberg resided in California on the filing\ndate, but the court held that \"residence alone does not amount\nto domicile\"\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: A person can reside in one state while domiciled\nin another, as Zuckerberg did in New Hampshire and Massachusetts\nduring school and in California in summer 2004\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: ConnectU LLC v. Zuckerberg\n\n[0m[1mCitizenship[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The state a party belongs to for diversity\npurposes; for an individual it is determined by domicile, and\nNote 1 says entities such as LLCs can be citizens of more than\none state at a time\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: The court found that both ConnectU and\nZuckerberg were citizens of New York at filing\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: The rules for the citizenship of corporations and\nLLCs are not explained in the attached reading\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: ConnectU LLC v. Zuckerberg\n\n[0m[1mTime-of-filing rule[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The principle that \"the jurisdiction of the Court\ndepends upon the state of things at the time of the action\nbrought,\" which Grupo Dataflux calls \"hornbook law\"\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It fixed September 2, 2004, as the date for\ndeciding Zuckerberg's domicile, which made his later move into\nCalifornia life irrelevant except as evidence of his intent on\nthat date\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It controls when citizenship is measured, not how\nit is determined\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: ConnectU LLC v. Zuckerberg\n\n[0m[1mRule 12(b)(1) motion[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A motion to dismiss for lack of subject matter\njurisdiction\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: This is the motion the Facebook Defendants filed\nto argue that diversity did not exist as a matter of fact\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: The defendants' motion raised several grounds,\nand the excerpt addresses only the subject matter jurisdiction\nground\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: ConnectU LLC v. Zuckerberg\n\n[0m[1mReport and Recommendation[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A magistrate judge's proposed findings and\nrecommended disposition of a motion referred under 28 U.S.C. §\n636(b)(1)(B), which a district judge may then adopt\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: Magistrate Judge Collings wrote the opinion as a\nReport and Recommendation, and District Judge Woodlock entered\nan order adopting it\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: The magistrate judge's report is a\nrecommendation, and the district judge's adopting order is the\ncourt's action\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: ConnectU LLC v. Zuckerberg\n\n[0m[1mEvidentiary hearing[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A proceeding in which the court takes testimony\nand evidence to resolve disputed facts\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It was held on June 22, 2006, to resolve\nZuckerberg's domicile, and the court relied on and credited his\ntestimony\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It was limited to the jurisdictional question and\nwas not a trial of the merits\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: ConnectU LLC v. Zuckerberg\n\n[0m[1mJurisdictional discovery[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Limited discovery that the court allows only on\nfacts relevant to jurisdiction\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: The parties were \"granted leave to conduct\nlimited discovery\" on Zuckerberg's citizenship when the original\ncomplaint was filed, which included his April 25, 2006\ndeposition\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Its scope is narrower than full merits discovery\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: ConnectU LLC v. Zuckerberg\n\n[0m[1mCo-location facility